CONFIG_IP_PNP_DHCP
CONFIG_EXT4_FS
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT

# The netfilter surface ze's own firewall programs. A fragment line asking for
# =y is a request; kconfig answers it, and it answered =m for the four tristate
# expressions below until NF_CONNTRACK and NF_NAT were themselves built in.
# Nothing reported that, because enforce_required_symbols (tools/kernel-builder/build.py)
# only checks symbols listed HERE, and none of these were listed. The result
# shipped for months: a kernel whose "inet" family is absent, so control-plane
# policing (coppTable, internal/plugins/copp/translate.go) makes a table the
# kernel refuses with EOPNOTSUPP.
#
# These entries make that failure loud and early. The build stops and names the
# symbol, instead of a daemon exiting at startup on the appliance.
CONFIG_NF_TABLES
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_INET
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_IPV4
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_IPV6
CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK
CONFIG_NF_NAT
CONFIG_NFT_CT
CONFIG_NFT_NAT
CONFIG_NFT_MASQ
CONFIG_NFT_REDIR
CONFIG_NFT_LIMIT
CONFIG_NFT_LOG
CONFIG_NFT_REJECT

# The list above is derived, not chosen: it is every nft expression the backend
# emits that still has a Kconfig symbol of its own in 7.x. `expr.Ct`, `expr.NAT`,
# `expr.Masq`, `expr.Redir`, `expr.Limit`, `expr.Log` and `expr.Reject` all appear
# in internal/plugins/firewall/nft, and every one of those symbols is tristate, so
# every one can silently answer =m.
#
# Deliberately NOT pinned, so the next reader does not read the gap as an
# oversight:
#   CONFIG_NFT_COMPAT   xtables compatibility. ze speaks netlink directly and
#                       emits no xtables target, and the symbol resolves to =m
#                       today, so pinning it would fail the build for a feature
#                       nothing uses.
#   CONFIG_NFT_HASH, CONFIG_NFT_FIB*, CONFIG_NFT_DUP*
#                       ze emits none of these expressions. NFT_DUP_IPV4 and
#                       NFT_DUP_IPV6 are asked for as =y in kernel.config and
#                       answer =m; that mismatch predates this list and is
#                       harmless only because nothing lowers a `dup`.
#   CONFIG_NFT_COUNTER, CONFIG_NFT_PAYLOAD, CONFIG_NFT_META, CONFIG_NFT_RBTREE,
#   CONFIG_NFT_OBJREF   no longer exist. Upstream folded them into nf_tables, so
#                       they are absent from the resolved .config rather than
#                       unset, and a pin on one could never be satisfied.
